# The Gauss-Bonnet theorem, tying the local bending of a surface to its global shape. For a closed surface the total
# curvature is two pi times the Euler characteristic, a topological invariant blind to how the surface bends. On a
# polyhedron the curvature sits at the vertices as the angle defect, the shortfall of the angles meeting there from a full
# turn, and the defects sum to two pi times the Euler characteristic every time, so the five Platonic solids, all spheres
# with Euler characteristic two, each carry a total defect of four pi. This file MODELS the five Platonic solids and the
# duality that swaps vertices with faces, and PROVES that duality is an involution: dualizing twice returns the original
# solid, and the tetrahedron is its own dual. It then records, as machine-integer counts, Euler's relation for each solid
# in the addition-only form vertices plus faces equals edges plus two, and the total angle defect as six times the Euler
# characteristic in units of a third of a straight angle. The duality theorem is quantified over the actual five-element
# type and proved by induction; the counts are held as exact integer facts.

# The five Platonic solids.
form solid
  case tetrahedron
  case cube
  case octahedron
  case dodecahedron
  case icosahedron

# The polyhedral dual, swapping vertices and faces: the tetrahedron is self-dual, the cube and octahedron are a dual
# pair, and the dodecahedron and icosahedron are a dual pair.
task dual
  take s, like solid
  like solid
  fork case, read s
    case tetrahedron
      send back
        make tetrahedron
    case cube
      send back
        make octahedron
    case octahedron
      send back
        make cube
    case dodecahedron
      send back
        make icosahedron
    case icosahedron
      send back
        make dodecahedron

# DUALITY IS AN INVOLUTION: dualizing twice returns the original solid, dual (dual s) = s, for every solid. So duality is
# a perfect pairing of the five solids, swapping the roles of vertices and faces while fixing edges. Proved by induction
# over the five solids.
rule duality-is-an-involution
  seat s, like solid
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call dual
        call dual
          read s
      read s
  fold s

# THE TETRAHEDRON IS SELF-DUAL: it is the fixed point of the duality, dual tetrahedron = tetrahedron, its four vertices
# and four faces interchangeable.
rule the-tetrahedron-is-self-dual
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call dual
        make tetrahedron
      make tetrahedron
  calm hold

# EULER'S RELATION as machine-integer counts, in the addition-only form vertices plus faces equals edges plus two, which
# is V - E + F = 2 with the subtraction cleared. The tetrahedron, 4 vertices, 4 faces, 6 edges: 4 + 4 = 6 + 2.
hold tetrahedron-euler-relation
  call is-equal
    call add
      code 4
      code 4
    call add
      code 6
      code 2

# The cube, 8 vertices, 6 faces, 12 edges: 8 + 6 = 12 + 2.
hold cube-euler-relation
  call is-equal
    call add
      code 8
      code 6
    call add
      code 12
      code 2

# The icosahedron, 12 vertices, 20 faces, 30 edges: 12 + 20 = 30 + 2.
hold icosahedron-euler-relation
  call is-equal
    call add
      code 12
      code 20
    call add
      code 30
      code 2

# THE TOTAL ANGLE DEFECT as six times the Euler characteristic, in units of a third of a straight angle where a full turn
# is six units. The tetrahedron, four vertices each short by three units, totals twelve, which is six times the Euler
# characteristic two: 4 * 3 = 6 * 2.
hold tetrahedron-total-defect-is-two-pi-euler
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 3
    call multiply
      code 6
      code 2

# The icosahedron, twelve vertices each short by one unit, totals twelve, again six times the Euler characteristic two:
# 12 * 1 = 6 * 2.
hold icosahedron-total-defect-is-two-pi-euler
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 12
      code 1
    call multiply
      code 6
      code 2
